22:58:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a very understandable concern, and you're right that a single test score can't capture the full complexity of a student's potential or a school's overall environment. But I'd argue that the key word here is "single." No responsible educator or policymaker relies on just one test score in isolation. The real power of standardized tests comes from patterns and aggregates. Think of it this way: a single blood pressure reading doesn't tell you everything about a person's health—it could be high because they just ran up a flight of stairs. But a series of readings over time, or readings from many patients in a clinic, reveals very real trends. Similarly, a single standardized test score can be noisy, but when you look at year-over-year growth for a student, or compare average scores across a school or district, you start seeing meaningful signals about ability and quality. For example, if a school's students consistently score well below the state average in math, year after year, that's not just random noise. It's a strong indicator that something in the curriculum, instruction, or resources needs attention. Conversely, if a student's scores improve steadily from 4th to 8th grade, that tells us something real about their academic growth—even if their absolute score on any given day fluctuates slightly. Also, standardized tests don't have to be the *only* measure. They work best as part of a broader assessment system that includes grades, teacher observations, portfolios, and other metrics. But without a standardized component, you lose the ability to compare apples to apples. A teacher's "A" in one school might mean something very different in another. Standardized tests add that objective anchor. So I'd push back gently: it's not that a single test score tells you everything. It's that standardized tests, used properly and in context, tell you something *essential* that other measures cannot. And that something—comparability, objectivity, and scalability—is crucial for fairness and accountability.
