ool for measuring progress. And as for creativity—I agree that tests don't measure it. But that doesn't mean tests are useless. It means we need *other* tools to measure creativity. The mistake is not using tests; it's using only tests. A good education system uses standardized assessments alongside portfolios, project-based evaluations, teacher observations, and student self-assessments. Each tool captures a different piece of the puzzle. So to sum up: I'm not saying tests capture everything that matters. I'm saying they capture something that matters *deeply*—academic proficiency—and that without that piece, the picture of a student or a school is dangerously incomplete. That, to me, is what makes them effective.
